# Why hazel {#sec:why}

Hazel (*Corylus avellana*) is the most forgiving nut tree a small garden
can hold. It coppices willingly, tolerates a hedge's worth of shade, and
starts bearing in four or five years rather than the decade a walnut
expects. The catch is pollination: hazel is wind-pollinated and
self-incompatible, so a single tree in an otherwise hazel-free
neighbourhood will flower every February and set nothing at all.

:::{admonition #adm:pollination}
Plant at least two cultivars whose flowering periods overlap. Two trees
of the *same* cultivar are genetically identical and will not pollinate
each other.
:::

@fig:catkins shows the two flower types that make this necessary. The
male catkins are impossible to miss; the female flowers are a few
millimetres across and easy to walk past for years.

![Male catkins and female flowers on the same branch in
February.](catkins.png){#fig:catkins}

# Spacing and yield {#sec:spacing}

Spacing is the decision that is hardest to undo. At the four-metre
spacing usually recommended for orchard rows, a garden fits very few
trees; at two metres the canopy closes by year six and yield per tree
collapses even though yield per square metre holds up for a while
[@crowe2018].

Kernel yield per tree follows roughly

$$
Y = k \cdot A \cdot e^{-\alpha d}
$$ {#eq:yield}

where $A$ is the ground area available to the tree, $d$ is the shading
from its neighbours, and $k$ and $\alpha$ are cultivar constants.
@eq:yield is descriptive rather than predictive — the constants vary
enough between cultivars that it is best used to compare spacings for
one cultivar, not to forecast a harvest.

| Spacing | Trees in 100 m² | Yield per tree | Yield per 100 m² |
|:--------|----------------:|---------------:|-----------------:|
| 4 m     |               6 |         2.9 kg |          17.4 kg |
| 3 m     |              11 |         1.8 kg |          19.8 kg |
| 2 m     |              25 |         0.6 kg |          15.0 kg |
: Modelled kernel yield by spacing, after ten years. {#tbl:spacing}

@tbl:spacing is why three metres is the usual compromise: it gives up
little against the theoretical optimum and leaves room to walk between
the rows. The underlying figures follow [@crowe2018; @schmidt2021].

:::{collapsable summary="A note on squirrels"}
Netting does not work. A squirrel takes hazelnuts at the milk stage,
before the shell hardens and well before you would think of harvesting,
and it will strip a tree in an afternoon. The only reliable answers are
a dog or a very large number of trees.
:::

# Conclusion {#sec:conclusion}

Two cultivars, three metres apart, and patience. See @sec:why for the
pollination constraint, @adm:pollination for what that means when you
choose them, and @sec:spacing for the arithmetic. The pollination one is
what costs a year if you get it wrong.
